@@ -49,8 +49,8 @@
 <section class="plain"><div class="wrap text">
   <h2>The data it keeps</h2>
   <ul class="list">
-    <li>Passwords can stay out of the config file (an inline one is refused for SQL Server and for the actions and EXPLAIN logins, not for a PostgreSQL server's own login). You choose whether statement text is kept as is, with every literal replaced, or not at all. Whichever you choose, on SQL Server and on PostgreSQL, the password-shaped parts of the statements, plans and Extended Events reports it keeps are masked before they are stored: a PASSWORD, SECRET, KEY_SOURCE, IDENTITY_VALUE or IDENTIFIED BY value; a password parameter by name, and every value of a call of sp_addlogin, sp_password, sp_addlinkedsrvlogin or the application-role procedures by position; a connection string's or a URL's password; the -P of a sqlcmd, bcp, osql or isql command line; a key, passphrase or password given to pgcrypto or to SQL Server's passphrase, certificate and asymmetric-key functions; a private key block; a plan's value of a password parameter. A password kept as an ordinary value in a table's data is a literal like any other: only the setting that replaces every literal, or keeps no text, takes it out.</li>
-    <li>Known limits of that mask. A statement that a community procedure returns as an XML processing instruction (sp_WhoIsActive's formatted sql_text and sql_command, the DarlingData procedures' query text, a plan wrapped as <code>&lt;?query_plan ... ?&gt;</code>) is masked as the statement it is when the instruction is the whole value. SQL Server writes a <code>?&gt;</code> inside such an instruction as it is, so the value is not always XML, and four shapes are not covered in full: an instruction that stands inside a larger XML document and whose statement holds a <code>?&gt;</code> followed by <code>&lt;</code> (an XML declaration in a literal) ends there, and a credential after it can be kept; the same value cut off after such a <code>?&gt;</code>; a document with text right after an instruction; and a document that opens and ends with an instruction other than the XML declaration, which is read as one statement. pgvigil stores no document of these shapes today: a showplan, a deadlock graph and a blocked process report have none. A value stored by an earlier version is not masked again.</li>
+    <li>Passwords can stay out of the config file (an inline one is refused for SQL Server and for the actions and EXPLAIN logins, not for a PostgreSQL server's own login). You choose whether statement text is kept as is, with every literal replaced, or not at all. Whichever you choose, on SQL Server and on PostgreSQL, the password-shaped parts of the statements, plans and Extended Events reports it keeps are masked before they are stored: a PASSWORD, SECRET, KEY_SOURCE, IDENTITY_VALUE or IDENTIFIED BY value; a password parameter by name, and every value of a call of sp_addlogin, sp_password, sp_addlinkedsrvlogin or the application-role procedures by position; a connection string's or a URL's password; the -P of a sqlcmd, bcp, osql or isql command line; a key, passphrase or password given to pgcrypto or to SQL Server's passphrase, certificate and asymmetric-key functions; a private key block; a plan's value of a password parameter, and every constant of a plan statement whose text names one (<code>OPTION (RECOMPILE)</code> and a local variable compile the value into the plan, where nothing else names it). A password kept as an ordinary value in a table's data is a literal like any other: only the setting that replaces every literal, or keeps no text, takes it out.</li>
+    <li>Known limits of that mask. A statement that a community procedure returns as an XML processing instruction (sp_WhoIsActive's formatted sql_text and sql_command, the DarlingData procedures' query text, a plan wrapped as <code>&lt;?query_plan ... ?&gt;</code>) is masked as the statement it is when the instruction is the whole value. SQL Server writes a <code>?&gt;</code> inside such an instruction as it is, so the value is not always XML, and four shapes are not covered in full: an instruction that stands inside a larger XML document and whose statement holds a <code>?&gt;</code> followed by <code>&lt;</code> (an XML declaration in a literal) ends there, and a credential after it can be kept; the same value cut off after such a <code>?&gt;</code>; a document with text right after an instruction; and a document that opens and ends with an instruction other than the XML declaration, which is read as one statement. pgvigil stores no document of these shapes today: a showplan, a deadlock graph and a blocked process report have none. A value stored by an earlier version is not masked again. The plan rule goes by the parameter's name in the statement's own text: a value copied into a variable or a parameter of another name (<code>SET @h = HASHBYTES('SHA2_256', @password)</code>, or an sp_executesql parameter given <code>@password</code>) keeps the constants it is compiled into, and so does a name with a letter outside A to Z before the word (<code>@m&ouml;tpassword</code>); a name with such a letter after the word (<code>@password&Auml;</code>) keeps the plan's listed value of the parameter.</li>
     <li>Its own database gives each part its own role: the collector adds data only through its import functions, the web page reads (and records each action it runs), the alert sender holds a login of its own, and the sign-in check runs through one function nobody else can call.</li>
     <li>The web page has its own sign-in (argon2id password hashes, strict cookies, rate limits) and refuses to listen beyond the local machine without HTTPS.</li>
   </ul>
